# Fabric Inspector To Do

See `planned_work.md` for scope, dependencies, and acceptance criteria.

Major functional implementation is incomplete. Read-only inspection and basic
isolated draft editing exist; visual graph editing, safe generation, complete
validation, and explicit submission do not. Start with the GPT Luna handoff in
`planned_work.md`; the unchecked items below remain open, not completed work.

## GUI Reorganization And Separation

- [ ] Verify the devcontainer bootstrap and clean build in a real container.
- [ ] Test cross-container ROS discovery and service calls, including compatible domain, middleware, discovery, network, and recovery settings.
- [ ] Implement safe generation and visual graph editing in the existing Fabric tabs; keep unavailable features clearly identified.
- [ ] Complete descriptive tooltip coverage and verify Analysis Jobs workflows remain accessible.
- [ ] Test the reorganized navigation, tooltips, existing inspection workflows, sandbox safety, and standalone/rqt operation.

## 1. Live Fabric Snapshot

- [ ] Verify and complete the read-only snapshot contract for plan identity, lifecycle, capability instances, event connections, and availability states.
- [ ] Verify snapshot consistency across no active plan, transitions, and failures in the existing Fabric service.
- [ ] Keep planned connections distinct from confirmed runtime connections and expose unavailable or stale data honestly.
- [ ] Incorporate running-capability and provider dependency information without assuming missing instance identities.
- [ ] Test snapshot consistency, lifecycle transitions, service errors, and cleanup.

## 2. Read-Only Graph

- [ ] Regression-test existing Experience database browsing and proposal workflows.
- [ ] Verify the loaded-plan and live snapshot views, including all three graph levels, event and dependency relationships, and node and edge details.
- [ ] Verify refresh, filtering, navigation, and empty, disconnected, partial, and stale states.
- [ ] Test aggregation, repeated instances, event types, dependency identity, and Qt interactions.

## 3. Graph Editor

- [ ] Verify unsaved-draft handling during removal from a full rqt host.
- [ ] Expand parser-backed comparisons beyond the nested-control fixture and checked-in Fabric default plan; identify unsupported or lossy structures and additional real-world plan cases.
- [ ] Implement parameter validation only after defining how to handle incomplete or stale runnable metadata. Current pair discovery checks names only; the service has no content freshness marker, Fabric's validator checks required runtime-input satisfaction rather than general value/type validity, and unknown extra attributes are not rejected.
- [ ] Add node and event-edge editing; retain parameter editing, discovered runnable-pair selection, and draft load/save.
- [x] Add `/fabric/plan/validate` for the exact draft and invoke it from the Graph Editor; validation is non-dispatching, and edits invalidate prior results. The service runs configured validators, not general parameter type/value checks. Keep submission disabled and do not use `/submit` as a dry run.
- [ ] Test graph-to-plan conversion, invalid drafts, unsupported XML, and round trips.

## 4. Explicit Dispatch

- [ ] Review the exact draft and require explicit confirmation before calling `/fabric/plan/submit`.
- [ ] Display acceptance, rejection, plan ID, and lifecycle status; handle unavailable services and timeouts without automatic retry.
- [ ] Test that edits never dispatch implicitly and failures do not cause duplicate submissions.

## 5. Integration And Documentation

- [ ] Add focused server, message, inspector, and integration tests for the end-to-end workflow.
- [ ] Document service contracts, prerequisites, source limitations, safe operation, build commands, and examples.
- [ ] Verify standalone and rqt workflows with the existing database inspection and sandbox features.
